<div class="topics" id="topics">

<div class="topic" data-i="0"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i1"/></svg></span><span><b>How Fellowship is marked</b><small>Global rating by domain</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><p class="cs-lead">In the Fellowship exam you give a <b>global rating</b>: a score that shows the level of competence you saw. The pass mark is set by the <b>modified Angoff method</b>.</p>
<div class="label">The domains you will assess</div>
<div class="doms"><div class="dom"><b>Overall professional capability and patient care</b></div><div class="dom"><b>Knowledge and judgement</b></div><div class="dom"><b>Quality of response</b></div><div class="dom off"><b>Bedside manner</b><span>Only where there is direct patient interaction. Not scored in patientless or online stations.</span></div></div>
<div class="callout">The approved descriptors for every domain are in the Marking descriptors topic.</div></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="1"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i7"/></svg></span><span><b>The 0 to 10 scale</b><small>Six levels, and 6 is the standard</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><div class="scale"><div class="s0"><b>0</b><span>No assessable</span></div><div class="s2"><b>2</b><span>Seriously deficient</span></div><div class="s4"><b>4</b><span>Below standard</span></div><div class="s6"><b>6</b><span>Meets standard</span></div><div class="s8"><b>8</b><span>Above standard</span></div><div class="s10"><b>10</b><span>Outstanding</span></div></div>
<div class="callout"><b>6 is the expected standard for a competent Fellowship candidate. It is not outstanding performance.</b></div>
<ul class="pts"><li><b>0:</b> no assessable performance in the domain. Do not use it automatically for a single wrong answer.</li><li><b>2 and 4:</b> below the expected standard, with more knowledge or competence shown at 4.</li><li><b>8 and 10:</b> need progressively stronger evidence of independent judgement, depth, synthesis and handling of complexity.</li></ul></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="2"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i4"/></svg></span><span><b>Marking descriptors</b><small>What each level looks like, by domain</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><p class="cs-lead">These are the approved descriptors for each domain. Choose a domain, then compare what you saw with each level.</p><div class="tabs four" role="tablist"><button type="button" role="tab" id="dt0" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="dp0">Patient care</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="dt1" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="dp1">Knowledge</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="dt2" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="dp2">Response</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="dt3" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="dp3">Bedside</button></div><div class="tp on" id="dp0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dt0"><div class="label">Domain 1</div><b class="dn">Overall professional capability and patient care</b><p class="dsub">Personal · Professionalism · Surgical · Adaptability · Ability to deal with grey areas</p><div class="ladder"><div class="rw s2"><div class="sc">2</div><div><div class="rt">Seriously deficient</div><p>The candidate demonstrated incompetence in the diagnosis and clinical management of patients to a level which caused serious concerns to the examiner</p></div></div><div class="rw s4"><div class="sc">4</div><div><div class="rt">Below expected standard</div><p>The candidate failed to demonstrate competence in the diagnosis and clinical management of patients</p></div></div><div class="rw s6"><div class="sc">6</div><div><div class="rt">Meets expected standard <span class=pill>Expected standard</span></div><p>The candidate demonstrated competence and confidence in the diagnosis and clinical management of patients</p></div></div><div class="rw s8"><div class="sc">8</div><div><div class="rt">Above expected standard</div><p>The candidate demonstrated ability and confidence above the level of competence</p></div></div><div class="rw s10"><div class="sc">10</div><div><div class="rt">Outstanding</div><p>The candidate demonstrated ability and confidence very significantly above the level of competence</p></div></div></div></div><div class="tp" id="dp1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dt1"><div class="label">Domain 2</div><b class="dn">Knowledge and judgement</b><p class="dsub">Knowledge · Ability to justify · Clinical</p><div class="ladder"><div class="rw s2"><div class="sc">2</div><div><div class="rt">Seriously deficient</div><ul class="dl"><li>Did not get beyond default questions</li><li>Failed in most or all competencies</li><li>Poor basic knowledge, judgement or understanding, to a level of concern</li><li>Serious lack of knowledge</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s4"><div class="sc">4</div><div><div class="rt">Below expected standard</div><ul class="dl"><li>Difficulty in prioritising</li><li>Gaps in knowledge</li><li>Poor deductive skills</li><li>Poor higher order thinking</li><li>Significant errors</li><li>Struggled to apply knowledge, judgement and management</li><li>Variable performance</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s6"><div class="sc">6</div><div><div class="rt">Meets expected standard <span class=pill>Expected standard</span></div><ul class="dl"><li>Competent knowledge and judgement of common problems</li><li>Essential points mentioned</li><li>Instils confidence</li><li>No major errors</li><li>Logical approach to difficult problems</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s8"><div class="sc">8</div><div><div class="rt">Above expected standard</div><ul class="dl"><li>Ability to prioritise</li><li>Comfortable with difficult problems</li><li>Good decision making; demonstrated a good level of higher order thinking; provided supporting evidence and familiar with literature</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s10"><div class="sc">10</div><div><div class="rt">Outstanding</div><ul class="dl"><li>At ease with higher order thinking</li><li>Flawless knowledge plus insight and judgement</li><li>Understood the breadth and depth of the topic, and quoted from literature</li><li>High flyer</li><li>Strong interpretation and judgement</li></ul></div></div></div></div><div class="tp" id="dp2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dt2"><div class="label">Domain 3</div><b class="dn">Quality of response</b><p class="dsub">Communication skills · Organisation and logical thought process · Q: questions, A: answers, P: prompting</p><div class="ladder"><div class="rw s2"><div class="sc">2</div><div><div class="rt">Seriously deficient</div><ul class="dl"><li>Q: Does not get beyond default questions</li><li>A: Disorganised, confused or inconsistent answers, lacking insight</li><li>P: Un-persuadable; prompts do not work</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s4"><div class="sc">4</div><div><div class="rt">Below expected standard</div><ul class="dl"><li>Q: Frequent use of default questions</li><li>A: Confused or disorganised answers; hesitant and indecisive</li><li>P: Required frequent prompting</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s6"><div class="sc">6</div><div><div class="rt">Meets expected standard <span class=pill>Expected standard</span></div><ul class="dl"><li>Q: Answers competence questions correctly</li><li>A: Methodical approach to answers; has insight</li><li>P: Requires minimal prompting</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s8"><div class="sc">8</div><div><div class="rt">Above expected standard</div><ul class="dl"><li>Q: Answers difficult questions correctly</li><li>A: Demonstrates a clear thinking process to difficult questions and answers</li><li>P: Fluent responses without prompting</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s10"><div class="sc">10</div><div><div class="rt">Outstanding</div><ul class="dl"><li>Q: Stretches examiners; answers questions at advanced level</li><li>A: Confident, clear, logical and focused answers</li><li>P: No prompting necessary</li></ul></div></div></div></div><div class="tp" id="dp3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dt3"><div class="label">Domain 4</div><b class="dn">Bedside manner</b><p class="dsub">Applicable to clinicals with patients only</p><div class="ladder"><div class="rw s2"><div class="sc">2</div><div><div class="rt">Seriously deficient</div><ul class="dl"><li>Abrupt or brusque manner</li><li>Arrogant</li><li>Inappropriate attitude or behaviour</li><li>No empathy</li><li>Rough handling of patients</li><li>Totally inappropriate examination of either sex</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s4"><div class="sc">4</div><div><div class="rt">Below expected standard</div><ul class="dl"><li>Does not listen; patronising</li><li>No introduction</li><li>Unsympathetic</li><li>Unobservant of body language</li><li>Inappropriate examination of either sex</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s6"><div class="sc">6</div><div><div class="rt">Meets expected standard <span class=pill>Expected standard</span></div><ul class="dl"><li>Appropriate introduction</li><li>Appropriate examination of either sex</li><li>Considerate examination</li><li>Shows respect</li><li>Responds to patient or carer</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s8"><div class="sc">8</div><div><div class="rt">Above expected standard</div><ul class="dl"><li>Gains patient confidence quickly</li><li>Good awareness of the patient’s reaction</li><li>Puts the patient at ease quickly</li></ul></div></div><div class="rw s10"><div class="sc">10</div><div><div class="rt">Outstanding</div><p>Exceptional communication and relationship with patient or carer</p></div></div></div></div><p class="note">Domains are rated separately. Bedside manner is scored only where there is direct patient interaction.</p></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="3"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i5"/></svg></span><span><b>Worked example</b><small>Child with a suspected anastomotic leak</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><div class="scn"><div class="label">Clinical scenario</div><p>A child develops a suspected anastomotic leak following repair of esophageal atresia. The candidate is asked to assess the complication, explain the immediate management, discuss indications for intervention and justify the management plan.</p></div>
<p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:15px">Choose a domain to see what each score looks like for this scenario.</p>
<div class="tabs" role="tablist"><button type="button" role="tab" id="tb0" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="tp0">Patient care</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="tb1" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tp1">Knowledge</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="tb2" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tp2">Response</button></div><div class="tp on" id="tp0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tb0"><div class="label">Domain 1</div><b style="font:700 17px var(--display)">Overall professional capability and patient care</b><p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--muted)">Can the candidate prioritise the child's condition, make safe decisions, anticipate deterioration and show the expected level of independent specialist practice?</p><div class="ladder"><div class="rw s0"><div class="sc">0</div><div><div class="rt">No assessable performance</div><p>Gives no relevant response or is unable to engage with the clinical problem sufficiently for assessment.</p></div></div><div class="rw s2"><div class="sc">2</div><div><div class="rt">Seriously deficient</div><p>Fails to recognise the potential seriousness of the leak; overlooks instability or sepsis and proposes potentially dangerous management.</p></div></div><div class="rw s4"><div class="sc">4</div><div><div class="rt">Below expected standard</div><p>Recognises the complication but gives an incomplete or poorly prioritised plan. Important aspects of stabilisation, source control or escalation are missed.</p></div></div><div class="rw s6"><div class="sc">6</div><div><div class="rt">Meets expected standard <span class=pill>Expected standard</span></div><p>Prioritises stabilisation, assesses for sepsis, initiates appropriate supportive treatment and antibiotics, and recognises when drainage or operative intervention may be necessary.</p></div></div><div class="rw s8"><div class="sc">8</div><div><div class="rt">Above expected standard</div><p>Develops a well-prioritised plan based on the child's stability, leak severity and contamination; anticipates deterioration and identifies clear indications for escalation or intervention.</p></div></div><div class="rw s10"><div class="sc">10</div><div><div class="rt">Outstanding</div><p>Demonstrates exceptional clinical judgement, anticipates complex problems, integrates immediate and longer-term care, and makes independent, safe decisions even when the clinical situation is uncertain.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="tp" id="tp1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tb1"><div class="label">Domain 2</div><b style="font:700 17px var(--display)">Knowledge and judgement</b><p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--muted)">How deep and broad is the knowledge? Can the candidate interpret findings, understand the underlying problem and justify decisions?</p><div class="ladder"><div class="rw s0"><div class="sc">0</div><div><div class="rt">No assessable performance</div><p>Demonstrates no assessable relevant knowledge or gives no meaningful answer.</p></div></div><div class="rw s2"><div class="sc">2</div><div><div class="rt">Seriously deficient</div><p>Demonstrates major gaps in knowledge, misunderstands the complication or gives fundamentally incorrect explanations of its management.</p></div></div><div class="rw s4"><div class="sc">4</div><div><div class="rt">Below expected standard</div><p>Knows some basic principles but cannot adequately explain the causes, assessment or management options. Struggles to justify decisions.</p></div></div><div class="rw s6"><div class="sc">6</div><div><div class="rt">Meets expected standard <span class=pill>Expected standard</span></div><p>Explains the relevant pathophysiology, appropriate investigations and treatment options; distinguishes stable from unstable patients and justifies a reasonable management plan.</p></div></div><div class="rw s8"><div class="sc">8</div><div><div class="rt">Above expected standard</div><p>Demonstrates good depth of knowledge, weighs alternative strategies, explains the indications and limitations of investigations, and justifies decisions using sound clinical reasoning and relevant evidence.</p></div></div><div class="rw s10"><div class="sc">10</div><div><div class="rt">Outstanding</div><p>Demonstrates exceptional depth and breadth of knowledge, sophisticated judgement and critical appraisal of alternatives. Integrates evidence, anatomy, physiology and patient-specific factors into a nuanced management strategy.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="tp" id="tp2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tb2"><div class="label">Domain 3</div><b style="font:700 17px var(--display)">Quality of response</b><p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--muted)">How well does the candidate communicate reasoning, organise an answer, justify decisions, respond to probing questions and prioritise information?</p><div class="ladder"><div class="rw s0"><div class="sc">0</div><div><div class="rt">No assessable performance</div><p>Gives no coherent or assessable answer.</p></div></div><div class="rw s2"><div class="sc">2</div><div><div class="rt">Seriously deficient</div><p>Responses are confused, contradictory or largely irrelevant. Cannot explain the reasoning behind proposed management, even with appropriate questioning.</p></div></div><div class="rw s4"><div class="sc">4</div><div><div class="rt">Below expected standard</div><p>Gives fragmented or poorly organised answers. Important points are omitted, prioritisation is weak and frequent prompting is required.</p></div></div><div class="rw s6"><div class="sc">6</div><div><div class="rt">Meets expected standard <span class=pill>Expected standard</span></div><p>Gives a clear, logical and reasonably structured answer. Identifies the key management steps, explains the main decisions and responds appropriately to relevant follow-up questions.</p></div></div><div class="rw s8"><div class="sc">8</div><div><div class="rt">Above expected standard</div><p>Communicates fluently and systematically, prioritises important information, clearly justifies decisions and responds confidently to probing questions with minimal prompting.</p></div></div><div class="rw s10"><div class="sc">10</div><div><div class="rt">Outstanding</div><p>Gives exceptionally clear, concise and logically structured responses. Synthesises complex information, anticipates relevant questions, explains uncertainty and demonstrates insight without unnecessary prompting.</p></div></div></div></div>
<div class="key">These are calibration anchors, not a substitute for the station-specific marking scheme.</div></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="4"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i4"/></svg></span><span><b>Rate each domain on its own</b><small>One candidate, three different scores</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><p>Domains are rated independently. A candidate may know a lot but prioritise badly, or judge well but struggle to explain the reasoning.</p>
<div class="bars">
<div class="bar2" style="--s:var(--c3)"><div class="t"><span>Overall professional capability and patient care</span><em>4/10</em></div><div class="tr"><i style="width:40%"></i></div><p>Recognises the leak but fails to prioritise the unstable child appropriately.</p></div>
<div class="bar2" style="--s:var(--c2)"><div class="t"><span>Knowledge and judgement</span><em>8/10</em></div><div class="tr"><i style="width:80%"></i></div><p>Demonstrates good knowledge of the causes, investigations and treatment options.</p></div>
<div class="bar2" style="--s:var(--good)"><div class="t"><span>Quality of response</span><em>6/10</em></div><div class="tr"><i style="width:60%"></i></div><p>Gives a generally logical answer but needs prompting to organise and prioritise the management plan.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="key">The line on each bar marks 6, the expected standard.</div>
<div class="callout">Strong knowledge alone does not establish overall clinical competence, particularly when patient safety and independent decision-making are being assessed.</div></div></div>

<div class="topic" data-i="5"><button class="th" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="ic"><svg><use href="#i8"/></svg></span><span><b>Guidance for examiners</b><small>Seven rules for scoring</small></span><span class="ch"></span></button><div class="pn"><ul class="pts">
<li>Score 0 only when there is no assessable performance in the domain. Do not use it automatically for a single incorrect answer.</li>
<li>Scores 2 and 4 are below the expected standard, with increasing degrees of knowledge or competence.</li>
<li>Score 6 is the expected standard for a competent Fellowship candidate, not outstanding performance.</li>
<li>Scores 8 and 10 need progressively stronger evidence of independent judgement, depth, synthesis and handling of complexity.</li>
<li>Consider prompting, but do not use prompting alone to decide the score. Assess the quality of the answer and how much help was needed.</li>
<li>Record domain scores separately from the station's checklist or structured marking score, where applicable. Follow the approved examination rules for combining scores and deciding the final result.</li>
<li>Do not score bedside manner in a patientless or online station without direct patient interaction. You can still judge communication from the candidate's verbal responses.</li></ul></div></div>

